      <h2>Creator income is business income. Most creators don't treat it that way.</h2>
      <p>AdSense, brand deals, affiliate commissions, channel memberships, digital products, and merchandise are all business revenue — not side income, not hobby money. The IRS taxes them accordingly, and the rules for reporting, deducting, and structuring that income are different from a W-2 paycheck in almost every way.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">Most creators either overpay because they don't claim the deductions they're entitled to, or underpay because they don't know self-employment tax exists on top of income tax. Both problems are avoidable with the right preparation.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">Creator businesses also have a distinct expense profile — gear, software, studio space, contractor editors, brand trip travel — that a general tax preparer doesn't know how to handle confidently. Getting it right requires someone who understands both the tax code and the creator business model.</p>
