{# ── FAQ ── #}
<section class="bg-cloud">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:720px;">
    <span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
    <h2>Common questions about creator business taxes</h2>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Is my YouTube channel actually a business for tax purposes?</summary>
      <p>If you're earning income from your channel with the intent to make a profit, yes — the IRS treats it as a business. That means your income goes on Schedule C, you pay self-employment tax on your net profit, and your business expenses are deductible. The key factor is profit motive: if you're actively trying to grow revenue, you're a business, not a hobby. This distinction matters enormously for what you can deduct.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>What is self-employment tax and how much is it?</summary>
      <p>Self-employment tax covers Social Security and Medicare — the taxes that are split between employer and employee when you have a regular job. As a self-employed creator, you pay both sides: 15.3% on net profit up to the Social Security wage base ($176,100 for 2025), then 2.9% on anything above that. This is assessed before income tax and often surprises first-year creators who weren't expecting it.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>What creator expenses are deductible as business expenses?</summary>
      <p>Equipment used for content creation (cameras, lenses, microphones, lighting, tripods, gimbals), software and subscriptions (editing software, music licensing, stock footage, analytics tools), home office space used exclusively for content work, a portion of your internet bill, contractor payments to editors and designers, travel to brand events or creator conventions, and professional services like accounting. Each deduction must be ordinary and necessary for your creator business.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Do I need an LLC to run my creator business?</summary>
      <p>No — many creators operate as sole proprietors and file a Schedule C without any separate business entity. An LLC provides liability protection but doesn't change your federal tax treatment by default (a single-member LLC is still taxed as a sole proprietor). The tax structure decision — and whether an LLC or S-Corp election makes sense — depends on your income level and specific situation. I can walk you through the tradeoffs.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How do I handle income from multiple platforms?</summary>
      <p>All creator income goes on the same Schedule C regardless of which platform paid you — AdSense, Twitch, Patreon, affiliate networks, brand deal payments, and digital product sales are all reported together as business revenue. Each platform may send its own 1099 form, and I make sure every source is accounted for correctly without double-counting or omissions.</p>
    </details>

